WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:04.650
I think the question of failure
has to do with what our goals are.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.140
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:11.220
I want to talk about why people
fail at language learning.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.060
Now, if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.650
the bell for notifications.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.040
So what do I mean by that?

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.760
What do I mean when I say that
people fail at language learning?

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:29.310
Well, if I look at the example in
Canada, which is the situation I know

00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:34.620
the best, just about every English
speaking Canadian has French at school.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:40.320
Depending on the province it might be six
years, eight years, 10 years of French.

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:46.275
Very few of those kids graduate from
high school, able to speak French.

00:00:46.335 --> 00:00:47.235
Very few.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:51.805
I gather from what I hear in
other countries, English-speaking

00:00:51.824 --> 00:00:53.985
countries, that the same is true.

00:00:54.285 --> 00:00:59.055
Now, there are always some who ended
up speaking very well and, you know, go

00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:03.230
off to the country where the language
is spoken, but they are a very small

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.480
minority, the vast majority fail.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:11.330
Now, I don't know what the situation is
in other countries, but I lived in Japan.

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.190
Everyone has English at school.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.950
The vast majority don't
understand English.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.920
Uh, I traveled in Spain.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:19.760
It's the same.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.900
Now there's always a small number
of people who speak english

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.090
or other languages well, but
the majority of people don't.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:33.060
I suspect that the vast majority of
people who study languages, in fact,

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.850
don't end up speaking the language.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.490
So are they failures?

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:42.090
Again, it all depends on
how you define failure.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:48.420
So if I get back to the example of
English-speaking kids in Canada, most of

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.120
those kids passed their French at school.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.050
They didn't fail.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:57.179
They got seven out of 10, eight
out of 10, nine out of 10, whatever

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.780
most of them passed, they had to
pass to get out of high school.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.370
So they didn't fail in that sense,
but they failed in the sense that they

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.380
didn't learn to speak the language.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:14.280
Um, right now I've been at my Arabic
for three years and my Persian

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.600
for two, I can't say that I speak
either language particularly well.

00:02:19.635 --> 00:02:23.115
I really struggle now I,
my Farsi is now better.

00:02:23.115 --> 00:02:26.115
I've had the opportunity to use
it a bit here and there around,

00:02:26.625 --> 00:02:29.805
uh, Vancouver, North Vancouver,
especially West Vancouver.

00:02:30.855 --> 00:02:34.905
Arabic, uh, I'm now exploring
Egypt in Arabic with Mohammed, my

00:02:34.905 --> 00:02:38.805
tutor, and I do struggle in our
conversations, there is no question.

00:02:39.135 --> 00:02:41.535
But am I failing in the language?

00:02:41.565 --> 00:02:45.285
So here again, I'm not, I
don't think I'm failing because

00:02:45.285 --> 00:02:46.755
I'm enjoying what I'm doing.

00:02:47.144 --> 00:02:48.315
I understand more and more.

00:02:49.485 --> 00:02:55.005
So I think the question of failure
has to do with what our goals are,

00:02:55.575 --> 00:03:00.735
goals that we set for ourselves or
goals that other people set for us.

00:03:01.425 --> 00:03:06.075
Now, I suspect that in our English
language school system in Canada

00:03:06.255 --> 00:03:10.005
most of those students have only one
goal and that's to pass the test.

00:03:10.125 --> 00:03:13.565
They're not particularly
interested in speaking French.

00:03:14.535 --> 00:03:19.274
So, uh, they succeeded
based on their goal.

00:03:19.274 --> 00:03:24.165
They didn't succeed based on a goal
that sort of someone else expects of

00:03:24.165 --> 00:03:26.385
them, and that is the ability to speak.

00:03:27.734 --> 00:03:32.445
Maybe in our school system,
we should put the emphasis on

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.575
comprehension and not on speaking.

00:03:34.995 --> 00:03:37.424
Speaking implies speaking correctly.

00:03:37.785 --> 00:03:44.955
So once you attempt to speak or to write
you produce the language so that the

00:03:44.955 --> 00:03:50.655
teacher can correct you, give you marks,
or even when you're listening, the teacher

00:03:50.655 --> 00:03:56.715
is going to test you on your listening
comprehension, setting you up for failure.

00:03:56.775 --> 00:04:01.005
Setting you up for, you know,
things that you can't do.

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:08.714
If we simply said we're going to expose
kids in school to a lot of content, uh,

00:04:08.744 --> 00:04:14.445
hopefully content they're interested
in, or, you know, I sometimes think

00:04:14.445 --> 00:04:19.575
if, if, um, kids in the Canadian, you
know, English language, school system

00:04:19.575 --> 00:04:24.075
had nothing, but the 60 mini stories
and every year they only did the mini

00:04:24.075 --> 00:04:28.305
stories, at least at the end of it all,
they would understand the mini stories.

00:04:28.425 --> 00:04:30.735
You would think after 5, 6, 8 years.

00:04:30.855 --> 00:04:34.575
Right now, I'm not sure they would
understand the mini stories unless

00:04:34.635 --> 00:04:35.955
they've been in the French immersion.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:39.165
So actually they're achieving
very, very little in their French.

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:45.000
Despite all kinds of instruction
on subjunctive and the tenses and

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.930
things like that, which they try to
kind of hang onto in order to pass

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:50.340
their tests and then quickly forget.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.410
So I think part of the failure
in our school system is this

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.800
emphasis on producing the
language and using it correctly.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:04.664
In fact, the vast majority of
English speaking people, kids in

00:05:04.664 --> 00:05:06.945
Canada, have no need to speak French.

00:05:06.945 --> 00:05:08.985
They have no one to speak French with.

00:05:09.255 --> 00:05:11.745
So it's an unrealistic expectation.

00:05:12.344 --> 00:05:14.115
Maybe the same is true in Japan.

00:05:14.175 --> 00:05:17.895
The vast majority of Japanese people
have no one to speak English to.

00:05:17.895 --> 00:05:22.094
They have no real need to speak English,
except for that one time when maybe,

00:05:22.145 --> 00:05:27.395
maybe they'll travel to Europe or to,
you know, somewhere where else where

00:05:27.395 --> 00:05:29.375
they'll need English to communicate.

00:05:30.224 --> 00:05:33.195
But then it's too late because
they've spent all that time,

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:38.294
you know, trying to learn how to
speak and they still can't speak.

00:05:38.414 --> 00:05:41.835
Again, if the emphasis had been
on comprehension, if at least

00:05:41.835 --> 00:05:45.934
to have a very high level of
comprehension, the speaking will come.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.469
I'm absolutely convinced, but without that
solid base in comprehension, the speaking,

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:58.530
you know, they might kind of achieve an
ability to say a few things for the test

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.180
or to produce a few things for the test.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.599
But after that it's gone because
they don't understand fundamentally

00:06:03.599 --> 00:06:04.919
don't understand the language.

00:06:05.219 --> 00:06:11.640
So I think a big part of failure
is this emphasis on speaking and

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.140
producing the language, which is
unrealistic because realistically to

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:18.030
speak well, you have to speak a lot.

00:06:18.429 --> 00:06:22.590
The Majority of people who are learning
a language in an environment where they

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:27.390
don't have the opportunity to speak a lot,
they're not going to end up speaking well.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:33.510
So it's far more useful in my opinion,
to emphasize comprehension and let them

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:39.450
graduate from very simple, repetitive
material to movies, things of interest to

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:44.910
them where all the time, the emphasis on
comprehension, not comprehension that we

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:50.310
test people on just comprehension because
they're listening to things initially

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:54.230
that are repetitious, but then eventually
things that are of interest to them

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.170
that will reduce the degree of failure.

00:06:58.605 --> 00:07:02.565
Learners won't be measuring
themselves against something that

00:07:02.565 --> 00:07:06.765
is an unrealistic expectation, the
ability to speak the language well,

00:07:07.485 --> 00:07:12.195
they will be measuring themselves the
way I do with my Arabic and Persian.

00:07:12.375 --> 00:07:14.175
You know, am I still doing it?

00:07:14.505 --> 00:07:15.825
Am I enjoying it?

00:07:16.245 --> 00:07:20.385
Is it, does it correspond to
what I am interested in doing?

00:07:20.505 --> 00:07:24.225
And as long as all of those things
are true, even if I can't speak

00:07:24.225 --> 00:07:27.630
well, I'm meeting my objectives.

00:07:27.659 --> 00:07:32.610
So I'm not, uh, failing in the
language, but if the expectation

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:36.630
is that after six months I should
speak the language and then I can't,

00:07:36.659 --> 00:07:38.640
then I feel as if I have failed.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:45.510
So I think where I'm coming from on this
is that we need to change the objectives

00:07:46.349 --> 00:07:50.340
of language instruction, and put a
far greater emphasis on comprehension,

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.320
which means more listening and reading.

00:07:53.235 --> 00:07:56.085
Letting people enjoy whatever
they're able to do in the language.

00:07:56.105 --> 00:08:01.625
There's far less testing, far less,
you know, marking people down.

00:08:02.165 --> 00:08:07.535
And on that basis, I think people will
feel better about their language learning.

00:08:07.535 --> 00:08:10.805
And as a result, they will
learn better and we'll have less

00:08:10.895 --> 00:08:12.905
failure in language learning.

00:08:13.505 --> 00:08:15.065
I look forward to your comments.

00:08:15.395 --> 00:08:16.265
Thank you for listening.

00:08:16.325 --> 00:08:16.775
Bye for now.

